  "result": "I've written the revised record to `record.jsonl`. I couldn't find what made `holds` come back violated, so this edit may not fix it. The record keeps the same id, notion, formals and claimed atoms.\n\n- **Cause not found:** the coder's own gate runs stopped at a scope check on the shell's temporary output files, so they never tested the Lean. The verdict came from the harness's later run, which shows only the result. By hand, the coder's body and `holds` trace (car in `r1` only, then over both lanes, judged at t = 1000) work out to compliant. That suggests a mistake in how the trace builds the lanes, such as which neighbour is recorded on which side, but I couldn't confirm it.\n- **Scenarios rewritten:** they now state which lane is recorded as whose neighbour on which side. They also say the trace has an observation before the judged one, which is judged on its own. The `blind` scenario now says occupancy is unobserved at both observations, which matches masking `rel.occupies`. The old wording claimed only the previous overlap with `r2` was unobserved.\n- **Contract changes:** the event is recognized at the first observation where the car's footprint overlaps `r2`, and nothing before the previous observation is read. The concept is also unknown when there is no previous observation; the old wording didn't say this. The notion and its boundaries are otherwise unchanged.\n- **`used_by`:** it now lists both laws that call the concept, `NRS_484B_223_1b` and `NRS_484B_587_2b`.\n\nThe coder also reported a harness problem. The scope check treats the shell's own output files under `.runtime/tmp/...` as changes outside the concept's files. That needs fixing on the harness side, or every coding session will fail its gate runs the same way.",
